---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import ClusterSketch from '../components/ClusterSketch.astro';
import {modules,levelNames,levels} from '../data/modules';
import {labs} from '../data/labs';
---
<Base title="Local laboratories"><div class="page-pad labs-page">
 <header class="lab-hero"><div class="page-header"><h1>Make the<br/>model real.</h1><p>Break a workload. Trace a request. Recover the data. Take each idea into a real cluster you can explore, reset, and understand.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="/labs/kv-labs.zip" download><Icon name="download"/>Download the labs</a><a class="text-link" href="#setup" data-open-setup>Set up your cluster <Icon name="arrow" size={16}/></a></div></div><ClusterSketch/></header>
 <div class="lab-environment"><span><strong>20</strong> guided investigations</span><span><strong>kind</strong> 0.33.0</span><span><strong>Kubernetes</strong> 1.37</span></div>
 <details class="lab-setup" id="setup">
  <summary>
   <div><h2>Your local workbench</h2><small>Set up a three-node lab cluster on macOS or Windows with WSL 2.</small></div>
   <Icon name="chevron" size={20}/>
  </summary>
  <div class="lab-setup-body">
   <p>Install the tools for your machine first. Both setups use Docker Desktop, Bash, Python 3, <strong>kind v0.33.0</strong>, and <strong>kubectl v1.37.x</strong>.</p>
   <div class="lab-platforms">
    <section aria-labelledby="setup-macos">
     <h3 id="setup-macos">macOS</h3>
     <ol>
      <li>Install and start <a href="https://docs.docker.com/desktop/setup/install/mac-install/">Docker Desktop for Mac</a>. Choose Apple Silicon or Intel to match your Mac.</li>
      <li>Open Terminal. Check <code>python3 --version</code> and install <a href="https://www.python.org/downloads/macos/">Python 3 for macOS</a> if needed.</li>
      <li>Install <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kind/releases/tag/v0.33.0">kind v0.33.0</a> and <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/tools/install-kubectl-macos/#install-kubectl-binary-with-curl-on-macos">kubectl v1.37.x</a> on your PATH. Use the Darwin ARM64 binaries for Apple Silicon or Darwin AMD64 for Intel. Use the linked kubectl instructions for an explicit release such as v1.37.0.</li>
     </ol>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="setup-windows">
     <h3 id="setup-windows">Windows with WSL 2</h3>
     <ol>
      <li>In administrator PowerShell, run <code>wsl --install -d Ubuntu</code>. Restart if prompted and open Ubuntu to finish setup. Run <code>wsl -l -v</code> in PowerShell to confirm Ubuntu shows version 2. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/install">Microsoft’s WSL setup guide</a> covers existing installs and upgrades.</li>
      <li>Install <a href="https://docs.docker.com/desktop/setup/install/windows-install/">Docker Desktop for Windows</a> and start it in Linux containers mode. Turn on the WSL 2 engine and enable Ubuntu under <strong>Settings → Resources → WSL Integration</strong>. <a href="https://docs.docker.com/desktop/features/wsl/">Docker’s WSL setup guide</a> shows the settings.</li>
      <li>Switch to the Ubuntu terminal for every command below. In Ubuntu, run <code>sudo apt update</code> and <code>sudo apt install curl python3 unzip</code>, then install the Linux builds of <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kind/releases/tag/v0.33.0">kind v0.33.0</a> and <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/tools/install-kubectl-linux/#install-kubectl-binary-with-curl-on-linux">kubectl v1.37.x</a> on your PATH. Use Linux AMD64 for an x64 PC or Linux ARM64 for an ARM PC. Use the linked kubectl instructions for an explicit release such as v1.37.0.</li>
     </ol>
    </section>
   </div>
   <p>The full suite passed on an Apple Silicon Mac. Windows with WSL 2 is the recommended Windows setup.</p>

   <div class="lab-setup-step">
    <h3>1. Check your tools</h3>
    <p>Run these in Terminal on Mac or Ubuntu on Windows. Look for a Docker <strong>Server</strong> section, kind v0.33.0, a v1.37.x kubectl client, and a Python 3 version.</p>
    <CodeBlock><code>{`docker version
kind version
kubectl version --client
python3 --version
bash --version`}</code></CodeBlock>
    <p>Leave several GiB of free disk space for images and the cluster. If you configure Docker’s memory allocation, 6–8 GiB is a starting point for three nodes.</p>
   </div>

   <div class="lab-setup-step">
    <h3>2. Download the labs and create the cluster</h3>
    <p><a href="/labs/kv-labs.zip" download>Download the lab bundle</a> and extract it. On Windows, extract it inside your Ubuntu home directory, for example with <code>unzip /path/to/kv-labs.zip -d ~/kv-labs</code>. In your terminal, change to the extracted directory containing <code>kind.yaml</code>. Keep this terminal open so the labs keep using its dedicated kubeconfig.</p>
    <CodeBlock><code>{`export KUBECONFIG="$PWD/kv-lab.kubeconfig"
kind create cluster --name kv-lab --config kind.yaml --wait 180s
kubectl --context kind-kv-lab wait --for=condition=Ready nodes --all --timeout=180s
kubectl --context kind-kv-lab get nodes`}</code></CodeBlock>
    <p>Expect one control-plane node and two workers, all <code>Ready</code>. If <code>kv-lab</code> already exists, use its original lab kubeconfig or finish and clean up that cluster before creating another one.</p>
   </div>

   <div class="lab-setup-step">
    <h3>3. Run your first exercise</h3>
    <p>From the extracted directory, run <a href="#m02">the reconciliation lab</a>. Its runner creates the lab namespace and shows the ReplicaSet replacing a deleted Pod. The subshell leaves you in the bundle directory for the next exercise.</p>
    <CodeBlock><code>{`(
  cd m02 && bash run.sh && bash cleanup.sh
)`}</code></CodeBlock>
    <p>Each investigation includes its own requirements, expected results, and cleanup. Keep all files for an exercise together.</p>
   </div>

   <div class="lab-setup-step">
    <h3>4. Remove the lab cluster</h3>
    <p>When you finish all exercises, save any lab data you want to keep, then delete the disposable cluster from the same terminal.</p>
    <CodeBlock><code>kind delete cluster --name kv-lab</code></CodeBlock>
   </div>
   <div class="lab-links">
    <a class="text-link" href="/labs/kind.yaml" download><Icon name="download" size={16}/>kind.yaml</a>
    <a class="text-link" href="/labs/README.md" download><Icon name="download" size={16}/>Lab README</a>
   </div>
  </div>
 </details>
 <section aria-labelledby="investigations-title"><div class="section-heading"><h2 id="investigations-title">Choose an investigation.</h2><span class="muted" id="lab-results" role="status">{labs.length} exercises</span></div>
 <div class="lab-toolbar"><nav class="lab-filters" aria-label="Lab levels"><button type="button" data-level="all" aria-pressed="true">All <small>{labs.length}</small></button>{levels.map(level=><button type="button" data-level={level} aria-pressed="false">{levelNames[level]} <small>{modules.filter(m=>m.level===level).length}</small></button>)}</nav><label class="lab-search"><Icon name="search" size={17}/><span class="sr-only">Find an exercise</span><input id="lab-filter" type="search" placeholder="Find an exercise…" autocomplete="off"/></label></div>
 <p class="lab-empty" id="lab-empty" hidden>No matching exercises. Try a workload, resource, or another level.</p>
 <div class="lab-catalogue">{labs.map(lab=>{const module=modules.find(m=>m.id===lab.id)!;return <section class="lab-section" id={lab.id} data-level={module.level} data-search={`${module.title} ${lab.goal} ${module.level}`.toLowerCase()}><header><div><span class="lab-id">{lab.id} · {levelNames[module.level]}</span><h3>{module.title}</h3></div><a class="text-link" href={`/curriculum/#${lab.id}`}>Lessons <Icon name="arrow" size={15}/></a></header><p>{lab.goal}</p><details class="lab-details"><summary>Requirements &amp; expected result</summary><p><strong>Requires:</strong> {lab.requirements}</p><p><strong>Expected:</strong> {lab.expected}</p><p>{lab.notes}</p></details><details class="lab-details"><summary>Run, inspect &amp; clean up</summary><h4>Commands</h4><CodeBlock><code>{lab.commands}</code></CodeBlock><h4>Diagnose</h4><CodeBlock><code>{lab.diagnostics}</code></CodeBlock><h4>Clean up</h4><CodeBlock><code>{lab.cleanup}</code></CodeBlock></details><details class="lab-details"><summary>Exercise files <span class="muted">{lab.files.length} files</span></summary><ul>{lab.files.map(file=><li><a class="text-link" href={`/labs/${lab.id}/${file.name}`} download>{file.name}<Icon name="download" size={13}/></a></li>)}</ul></details></section>})}</div></section>
 </div>
 <script>
  const setup=document.querySelector<HTMLDetailsElement>('#setup')!;
  document.querySelectorAll('[data-open-setup]').forEach(link=>link.addEventListener('click',()=>setup.open=true));
  if(location.hash==='#setup')setup.open=true;
  const input=document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('#lab-filter')!;
  const filters=[...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.lab-filters button')];
  const entries=[...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.lab-section')];
  let level='all';
  function filterLabs(){const query=input.value.trim().toLowerCase();let count=0;for(const entry of entries){entry.hidden=!(level==='all'||entry.dataset.level===level)||!entry.dataset.search!.includes(query);if(!entry.hidden)count++;}for(const button of filters)button.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(button.dataset.level===level));document.querySelector('#lab-results')!.textContent=`${count} ${count===1?'exercise':'exercises'}`;document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#lab-empty')!.hidden=count>0;}
  input.addEventListener('input',filterLabs);
  for(const button of filters)button.addEventListener('click',()=>{level=button.dataset.level!;filterLabs();});
  window.addEventListener('hashchange',()=>{const entry=entries.find(e=>`#${e.id}`===location.hash);if(entry?.hidden){level='all';input.value='';filterLabs();entry.scrollIntoView();}});
 </script>
</Base>
